  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Community-based mental health, protection and Wash intervention for the most at-risk Syrian refugees and host community members, in particular older people and people with disabilities, in Lebanon</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Brief description of the outputs and activities:
(max. &#xBD; page)
The project will continue to provide humanitarian assistance to address critical gaps in mental health services and protection services specifically protection from gender-based violence (GBV) for the most vulnerable groups, in particular people with disabilities and older people, while contributing to building crisis resilience among Syrian refugees and host communities living in urban areas of Lebanon (Beirut, Mount Lebanon, and Northern governorates). 
Mental health services will be provided through IDRAAC clinic and the MHPSS hotline. IDRAAC outreach teams will identify, support and follow-up the most vulnerable and mobility-impaired beneficiaries through home visits, provide them with psychosocial support, and ensure they have access to health and protection services through referral mechanisms. Caregivers will also be direct beneficiaries from Mental Health and Psychosocial Support (MHPSS) interventions, and they will be provided with advice on how to support older persons or persons with disabilities. 
HelpAge will be leading the provision of multi-purpose cash assistance to the most-at-risk households identified through a vulnerability scoring system that indicates their eligibility for this activity based on specific criteria that will be set during the early stages of the project.
In response to the Cholera outbreak, HelpAge and IDRAAC will provide preventive WASH services to the targeted population including various awareness raising activities on Cholera, promotion of hygiene good practices and distribution of hygiene kits, and distribution of absorbent pads for older people in need.
Information and orientation sessions on protection issues, in particular age and GBV including violence and abuse against older people and people with disabilities, will be organised at community level and in the clinics. Sessions will aim at increasing understanding of the beneficiaries&apos; rights and informing them about services available. Through intergenerational and older people support groups, participants will be able to share their knowledge and experiences with each other and increase interactions and social cohesion in their own community. Survivors of age or GBV and at-risk persons will be identified, offered a safe space at the clinic and benefit from case management and psychosocial support services to help manage consequences of violence, abuse, and neglect. They will be referred to external health or protection service providers when needed. 
Trainings will be organised to increase awareness and capacities of partners, local community-based organisations, authorities, regional humanitarian actors, service providers and community members in relation to acute protection issues faced by the most vulnerable groups, in particular women, people with disabilities and older people. The training platform developed by HelpAge Jordan in 2019 and 2020 with support from GFFO will be a critical resource to ensure a wide reach.
The project will be implemented in close collaboration with local CBOs to ensure local ownership and sustainable impact of the action. Through this approach, it contributes to the resilience and peaceful co-existence of refugees and host communities and building trust within these communities to work together towards a safe and supportive community which also connects to the triple-nexus. HelpAge continuously works on innovative ways to build a bridge between humanitarian aid and long-term solutions of development.</narrative>
  </description>
