  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1: Most at-risk and vulnerable Syrian refugees and host community members, including older people and people with disabilities, and their caregivers, have increased access to critical mental health services.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2: The most at-risk Syrian refugees and host community members are aware of their rights and have increased access to protection services in response to issues of age and gender-based violence</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3: Community members, including older people, people with disabilities and care providers, as well as local authorities, service providers and humanitarian actors, have a better understanding of age, gender and disability inclusive programming.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4: The affected vulnerable families, including older people and people with disabilities, have improved access to Cholera preventive WASH services.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x25CF;	Older women and men, particularly those living alone and those with mobility impairment;
&#x25CF;	Women, men and children with disabilities;
&#x25CF;	Survivors of age and gender-based violence;
&#x25CF;	Members from refugee communities in particular Syrians, and non-registered refugees such as Palestinian and Iraqi, and others</narrative>
  </description>
